1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,265
Black Friday:

2
00:00:01,265 --> 00:00:02,931
checkout traffic triples.

3
00:00:02,931 --> 00:00:06,528
Pay for idle servers all year, or scramble on the day?

4
00:00:06,528 --> 00:00:07,660
This is KubeSage:

5
00:00:07,660 --> 00:00:12,990
an AI SRE team for Kubernetes, built for the Meta Global AI Developer Hackathon.

6
00:00:13,794 --> 00:00:15,713
FinOps and SRE, in one system.

7
00:00:15,713 --> 00:00:21,280
It plans savings, forecasts rushes from usage and your calendar, and fixes what breaks.

8
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:24,352
Small models do the legwork, so runs cost cents.

9
00:00:24,352 --> 00:00:26,463
Every finding must cite evidence.

10
00:00:26,463 --> 00:00:28,255
Agent fixes are git commits.

11
00:00:28,255 --> 00:00:32,734
And plans and fixes reach the cluster only when a person applies them.

12
00:00:33,539 --> 00:00:34,431
Meet shop-prod:

13
00:00:34,431 --> 00:00:36,867
a hypothetical store on Azure Kubernetes.

14
00:00:36,867 --> 00:00:40,909
Five services share one node, at two hundred eighty dollars a month.

15
00:00:40,909 --> 00:00:43,761
Two reserve two cores each, and sit mostly idle.

16
00:00:43,761 --> 00:00:45,960
Checkout peaks every weekday morning.

17
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:47,862
Black Friday is on the calendar.

18
00:00:47,862 --> 00:00:51,249
And tonight, a ledger cache ships with too little memory.

19
00:00:52,052 --> 00:00:55,142
So FinOps and engineering keep one business calendar:

20
00:00:55,142 --> 00:00:57,706
working hours, Black Friday, Christmas week.

21
00:00:57,706 --> 00:01:02,836
KubeSage reads it with thirty days of usage, and forecasts each rush with no model call:

22
00:01:02,836 --> 00:01:04,935
when to pre-warm, and how many pods.

23
00:01:04,935 --> 00:01:06,567
A flash sale nobody planned?

24
00:01:06,567 --> 00:01:08,024
A CPU trigger catches it.

25
00:01:08,024 --> 00:01:12,512
And for Black Friday, the plan asks for a one-day scale-up from seven thirty.

26
00:01:13,322 --> 00:01:15,539
Statistics and the forecast are free.

27
00:01:15,539 --> 00:01:18,117
One Muse Spark call triages every workload.

28
00:01:18,117 --> 00:01:23,091
Spark handles the routine fixes; Muse Wonder takes the hard case and the node pool.

29
00:01:23,091 --> 00:01:27,766
Guardrails floor requests from history and restore dropped calendar pre-warms.

30
00:01:27,766 --> 00:01:28,126
Total:

31
00:01:28,126 --> 00:01:31,602
twenty-seven cents, under half the price of all on Wonder.

32
00:01:32,404 --> 00:01:36,468
When an alert fires, four small agents gather evidence in parallel.

33
00:01:36,468 --> 00:01:38,591
A finding with no collected signal?

34
00:01:38,591 --> 00:01:39,076
Dropped.

35
00:01:39,076 --> 00:01:44,900
A larger Thinker reads the rest, plus your team's plain-English rules, and finds the root cause.

36
00:01:44,900 --> 00:01:49,814
The Executor writes the fix as a git commit, after policy checks limit each edit.

37
00:01:51,067 --> 00:01:52,830
Here's the simulated cluster.

38
00:01:52,830 --> 00:01:55,079
Each glass is the CPU a pod reserves.

39
00:01:55,079 --> 00:01:55,808
Hit Analyze:

40
00:01:55,808 --> 00:01:59,577
seventy percent less reservation cost, for twenty-seven cents.

41
00:01:59,577 --> 00:02:04,440
The forecast shows each service's biggest rush, with its pre-warm time and pods.

42
00:02:04,440 --> 00:02:08,817
And to fit scheduled peaks, the node pool costs thirty-two percent more.

43
00:02:10,461 --> 00:02:13,729
Apply right-sizes the services and adds the KEDA rules.

44
00:02:13,729 --> 00:02:16,521
It skips moving pods until the new nodes exist.

45
00:02:18,492 --> 00:02:19,866
Then, the bad release:

46
00:02:19,866 --> 00:02:24,362
ledger-cache runs out of memory, and the ContainerOOMKilled alert fires.

47
00:02:25,942 --> 00:02:27,575
The team jumps in on its own.

48
00:02:27,575 --> 00:02:32,192
Sub-agents report, the Thinker finds the cause, and the chat shows every hand-off.

49
00:02:34,109 --> 00:02:35,432
The fix is a commit:

50
00:02:35,432 --> 00:02:40,394
more memory, a memory-optimised node pool proposal, and two security fixes.

51
00:02:40,394 --> 00:02:41,452
Sixteen seconds.

52
00:02:41,452 --> 00:02:42,114
Two cents.

53
00:02:42,114 --> 00:02:43,239
A person decides.

54
00:02:44,132 --> 00:02:45,171
That's KubeSage:

55
00:02:45,171 --> 00:02:51,082
FinOps and SRE in one system, built on Muse Spark and Muse Wonder, with a person in charge.
